      <p>Performance Max is sold as a campaign you switch on and leave alone. Left alone is exactly how it goes wrong.</p>
      <p>Its default behaviour is to find the cheapest conversions available, and the cheapest conversions in almost any account are the people who were going to buy anyway. It takes them, reports them as new business, and the dashboard looks excellent while the business does not change.</p>

      <h2>The brand problem</h2>
      <p>Without brand exclusions, Performance Max bids on searches for your own company name. Those convert cheaply, so the campaign's average cost per acquisition drops and its return on ad spend rises.</p>
      <p>None of that is new demand. Most of those people would have found you through the organic listing for free. The campaign has not grown anything, it has moved existing revenue into a line item you now pay for.</p>
      <p>The first thing we do on any Performance Max account is exclude brand terms and re-baseline the numbers. Reports usually look worse afterwards. They are also finally true.</p>

      <h2>What we actually do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Asset groups by product line or service</strong>, not everything dumped into one. One asset group means one message for every product, and no way to tell which is working.</li>
        <li><strong>Brand exclusions</strong>, applied at account and campaign level, so new customer acquisition is measurable.</li>
        <li><strong>Audience signals from first-party data</strong> where it exists. Customer lists and converter data beat broad interest guesses.</li>
        <li><strong>Search themes</strong> set deliberately rather than left blank.</li>
        <li><strong>Listing group segmentation</strong> for retail accounts, so margin is visible in the structure rather than averaged away.</li>
        <li><strong>Channel and placement reporting</strong> extracted through scripts and reports, because the interface does not show where impressions went by default.</li>
        <li><strong>New customer acquisition goals</strong> configured where the business genuinely wants new buyers rather than repeat ones.</li>
        <li><strong>Budget guardrails</strong>, so Performance Max does not quietly absorb budget from Search campaigns that were working.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Seeing inside it</h2>
      <p>The common complaint is that Performance Max is a black box. It is more opaque than Search, but less opaque than people assume.</p>
      <p>Placement data, search category data, asset performance and channel splits can all be pulled out with reports and scripts. That is the difference between running Performance Max and letting it run you. If an agency cannot tell you which channel your Performance Max spend went to, they are not managing it.</p>

      <h2>When Performance Max is the wrong choice</h2>
      <p>It is not right for everything, and the honest answer saves money.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Not enough conversion volume.</strong> Performance Max needs conversions to learn. A handful a month is not enough, and the campaign will spend while it waits.</li>
        <li><strong>Tracking is not reliable.</strong> It optimises towards whatever you tell it a conversion is. Fix measurement first or it will efficiently buy the wrong thing.</li>
        <li><strong>Brand is the whole account.</strong> If nearly all demand is branded, Performance Max mostly rebuys what you already had.</li>
        <li><strong>You need control over placements.</strong> Some regulated or sensitive categories cannot accept the inventory it uses.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In those cases Search, properly structured, usually does more. We would rather say that than sell you a campaign type.</p>

      <h2>Performance Max alongside Search</h2>
      <p>The two compete if you let them. Performance Max will take Search traffic and report it as its own, which makes Search look weaker than it is and tempts people into cutting the campaign that was actually working.</p>
      <p>Keeping them separate takes brand exclusions, deliberate negative lists, and reporting that compares like with like. It is ongoing work, not a setting.</p>

